# QuantLab 0DTE ML Cursor Rule

Read these files before changing code:

- `AGENTS.md`
- `ROADMAP.md`
- `README.md`
- `QUANTLAB_0DTE_ML_CURSOR_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`

## Phase control

- Execute only the phase explicitly requested by the user.
- Do not proceed to the next phase.
- End every phase with files changed, commands run, test results, assumptions, risks, and acceptance-gate status.
- Do not make broad unrelated refactors.
- Use a feature branch, not `main`.

## Architecture

Preserve:

```text
data/       network and storage
quality/    validation
factors/    pure deterministic calculations, no network I/O
terminal/   API and payload assembly
ml/         datasets, training, calibration, registry, inference
```

Do not duplicate an existing abstraction without first documenting why it cannot be extended.

## Financial rules

- Dealer positioning is model-implied, not observed.
- Open interest does not identify dealer direction.
- Keep sign assumptions in metadata.
- Use exact point-in-time time to expiry for 0DTE.
- Separate SPX AM settlement and SPXW PM settlement.
- Do not silently change Pin/GEX/VEX definitions.
- Do not add automatic trade execution.

## No-lookahead rules

At timestamp `t`, use only source events with timestamp `<= t`.

Never use:

- final daily option volume;
- quotes/trades/Greeks after `t`;
- next-day OI;
- closing data to recompute an earlier Pin zone;
- post-release macro results before the release;
- a strike universe selected from the future close.

Train/validation/test splits must group by complete trading session. Never use random row-level splitting.

## Data rules

- Raw source data is immutable.
- Use Parquet for market-data storage.
- Use atomic writes, manifests, checksums, and schema versions.
- Never log or commit credentials.
- Do not overwrite incomplete partitions silently.
- Preserve source timestamps and data-age fields.
- Do not call NBBO a full L2 order book unless the capability audit proves it.

## Modeling rules

- Complete transparent baselines before deep fusion.
- Advanced models are candidates, not presumed winners.
- Use proper scoring rules and probability calibration.
- Do not select by accuracy alone.
- Keep a locked final holdout.
- Use OOF predictions for stacking.
- Reject complexity that does not produce stable walk-forward gains.
- Add model, dataset, feature, and label version metadata to every artifact.

## Deployment rules

- Local RTX GPU is for training/teacher inference.
- Railway must remain CPU-safe.
- Railway must have a deterministic fallback if the student model is missing.
- Every prediction must identify model version, schema, timestamp, quality, and prediction source.
- Do not expose a local laptop via an unauthenticated public endpoint.

## Required validation

Before marking work complete:

1. run relevant unit tests;
2. run full pytest when practical;
3. run ruff;
4. report exact commands and results;
5. state whether the phase acceptance gate passed.
